latest_plan = "Based on our reflection, here's a step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Create a deep copy of the input grid to work on.\n\n2. Scan the entire grid to identify all initial sky blue (8) cells. Store their coordinates in a list.\n\n3. Create a set to store the coordinates of all cells that should become sky blue in the final grid.\n\n4. For each initial sky blue cell:\n   a. Calculate the coordinates of the 3x3 square centered on this cell.\n   b. For each cell in this 3x3 square:\n      - If the cell is within the grid boundaries and is either black (0) or blue (1), add its coordinates to the set of cells to be changed.\n\n5. Iterate through the set of cells to be changed:\n   - For each coordinate in the set, change the corresponding cell in the copied grid to sky blue (8).\n\n6. Return the modified grid.\n\nThis plan addresses the key aspects we identified:\n\n- It maintains the 3x3 square pattern of expansion.\n- It respects grid boundaries and only replaces black (0) and blue (1) cells.\n- It allows for merging of expanding regions by using a set to store coordinates to be changed.\n- It simulates simultaneous expansion by collecting all changes before applying them.\n\nHere's a more detailed breakdown of the implementation:\n\n```python\ndef solve_4ff4c9da(input_grid: ColoredGrid) -> ColoredGrid:\n    # Step 1: Create a deep copy of the input grid\n    new_grid = input_grid.deep_copy()\n    rows, cols = new_grid.get_dimensions()\n\n    # Step 2: Identify all initial sky blue cells\n    sky_blue_cells = [\n        (r, c) for r in range(rows) for c in range(cols)\n        if input_grid.get_cell(r, c) == 8\n    ]\n\n    # Step 3: Create a set to store coordinates to be changed\n    cells_to_change = set()\n\n    # Step 4: Calculate cells to be changed\n    for r, c in sky_blue_cells:\n        for dr in [-1, 0, 1]:\n            for dc in [-1, 0, 1]:\n                nr, nc = r + dr, c + dc\n                if 0 <= nr < rows and 0 <= nc < cols:\n                    if input_grid.get_cell(nr, nc) in [0, 1]:  # black or blue\n                        cells_to_change.add((nr, nc))\n\n    # Step 5: Apply changes\n    for r, c in cells_to_change:\n        new_grid.set_cell(r, c, 8)\n\n    # Step 6: Return the modified grid\n    return new_grid\n```\n\nThis implementation should correctly solve the challenge for all given test cases, capturing the 3x3 expansion pattern and handling multiple sky blue regions and their potential merging."
